 a backtracking algorithm, reducing the number of nodes explored in the search tree is sufficient to guarantee an improvement in the overall running time."}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"True","correct":false,"option_id":"2ea88dd7-8629-4db8-9fed-0165e8eac769"},{"type":"text","content":"False","correct":true,"option_id":"2efb549a-d72d-4e72-aed9-96474265dfef"}]}}},{"path":"versions/382f0ac1-5ecb-4c31-b0b1-afaea19e192c/044630cd0c8deba4224d1ad880cc56e58a9dae3d524bd0607dc896d67568527b.json","value":{"schema_version":1,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"382f0ac1-5ecb-4c31-b0b1-afaea19e192c","revision":"sha256:044630cd0c8deba4224d1ad880cc56e58a9dae3d524bd0607dc896d67568527b","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"A divide-and-conquer algorithm divides a problem of size $n$ into 3 subproblems, each of size $\\frac{n}{2}$. The time required to divide the problem and combine the results is $\\Theta(n\\log n)$. The overall time complexity of this algorithm is $\\Theta(n^{\\log_2 3})$."}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"True","correct":true,"option_id":"ba0e7d4e-a455-43d7-b05e-21f69eb05cb1"},{"type":"text","content":"False","correct":false,"option_id":"8eec8f15-da36-4d3b-91b7-0dbaf28562a4"}]}}},{"path":"versions/349b59fe-abc0-48cd-a56f-e67de7e3d7b7/079000540c5bd289acb1871110ab4f00559283e70e14a9e1c8c7d1bb222e121e.json","value":{"schema_version":1,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"349b59fe-abc0-48cd-a56f-e67de7e3d7b7","revision":"sha256:079000540c5bd289acb1871110ab4f00559283e70e14a9e1c8c7d1bb222e121e","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"Starting from an initially empty Binomial Queue, performing 58 consecutive Insert operations requires a total of 54 pairwise links of binomial trees of the same degree."}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"True","correct":true,"option_id":"070d1f69-